# Code owners for the governance control plane.
#
# Purpose: stop an amendment or a guardrail change from auto-merging before a
# human has actually looked at it. PR #1098 rebased, went green, and squashed
# itself in while review corrections were still being written — required status
# checks cannot tell a reviewed amendment from an unreviewed one, only a required
# review can.
#
# IMPORTANT: this file is advisory on its own. It takes effect only once master's
# branch protection has "Require a pull request before merging" +
# "Require review from Code Owners" enabled.
#
# Solo-maintainer caveat: GitHub does not let you approve your own pull request.
# With code-owner review required, a PR you authored yourself cannot be approved
# by anyone else here — you merge it via admin override instead. That is the
# intended shape: auto-merge stops being able to land governance changes silently,
# and merging becomes a deliberate act. Leave "Do not allow bypassing the above
# settings" OFF, or you will lock yourself out of your own amendments.

# The constitution itself.
/.specify/                          @steiner385

# The guardrails that enforce it.
/.claude/managed-settings.json      @steiner385
/.claude/hooks/                     @steiner385
/.claude/settings.json              @steiner385
/.codex/config.toml                 @steiner385
/.codex/rules/                      @steiner385
/server/sdlc/governance.py          @steiner385
/server/sdlc/constitution_gate.py   @steiner385
/scripts/check_constitution_amendment.py @steiner385
/.github/workflows/constitution.yml @steiner385
/.github/CODEOWNERS                 @steiner385

# Every workflow and repo-automation file. Once the repo is public, an agent PR that
# auto-merged a new workflow (e.g. `pull_request_target`, or `issue_comment` with a
# write token) could undo the "no dangerous triggers" property CI depends on, so any
# change under .github/ needs a human's review, like the governance files above.
/.github/                           @steiner385
